    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d862">

                <pb facs="0935" n="649" xml:id="pg649"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 341.622a/150a</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the Ambassador in Great
                        Britain</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Page</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Telegram]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1916-06-08T16:00:00-05:00">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">June 8, 1916, 4 p. m.</hi>
                        </date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>3402. Your 4335<anchor n="1" xml:id="d862-f1"/> May 20. Please take up the <hi
                        rendition="#italic">China</hi> case again with Sir Edward Grey, and tell him
                    that I am anxious to know at the earliest moment when the persons will be
                    released and what arrangements are being made for their transportation to their
                    destination, as indicated in your note to him. Nearly three weeks have now
                    elapsed without any reply and I can not allow the settlement of this important
                    case to be unduly delayed.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Lansing</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
